(* loop_static2.rwhile -- a subject that CONTAINS A LOOP, kept to three variables
 * so ri_fp3 (which dispatches over exactly V0/V1/V2) can interpret it.
 *
 * WHY IT EXISTS (2026-08-09).  The jones-self battery had no loop subject at
 * all: every one of id/id2/id3/rep/swap/sx_splitjoin/sx_three is loop-free, so
 * the reversible Jones criterion (resid <= p+) had only been tested on straight
 * line code.  The obvious loop subject, examples/reverse.rwhile, cannot be
 * specialised through ri_fp3 (spec_av raises 'error <= '41 -- see
 * FINDINGS_reversible_projections.md), because its loop's EXIT test reads the
 * dynamic input.  This one separates the two things that failure conflates:
 * having a loop, and having DATA-DEPENDENT loop control.
 *
 * The loop control here is entirely STATIC -- the counter V1 starts nil and the
 * exit test compares it with a literal -- while the loop BODY operates on the
 * dynamic input V0.  So spec_av's static unrolling ('lcheck) applies and the
 * residual should be loop-free, which is the property the Jones measurement is
 * about.  A data-dependent loop is the open case, not this one.
 *
 * [loop_static2](d) = ((d.nil).nil)  -- two iterations, so the counter really is
 * consulted twice and a one-iteration bug would show.
 *
 * loop_static3.rwhile is the same program with one more iteration.  The PAIR is
 * the point: it says whether the residual's cost per unrolled iteration is
 * constant, i.e. whether `resid <= p+` survives deeper unrolling or only holds
 * because the loop was short.
 *
 * The counter must be cleared before `write`: R-WHILE's store invariant is that
 * every variable but the output one ends nil. *)
read V0;
  from  =? V1 nil
  do    V0 <= cons V0 nil
  loop  V1 <= cons nil V1
  until =? V1 (nil . nil);
  V1 ^= (nil . nil);
write V0
